const QUESTIONS = [
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A pipeline must fetch a report, summarize it, and email the summary — the same three steps, in the same order, every single run. Which architecture fits?",o:["A manager/subagent hierarchy coordinating three separate specialists","A predefined workflow, since the sequence and order are fixed and known ahead of time","A single call with adaptive thinking enabled and no other structure","An autonomous agent that re-derives its own steps on each run"],c:[1],e:"When the steps and their order are already known, code can hard-code the sequence — that predictability is exactly what a workflow buys you."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A research assistant needs to investigate a claim, and the number of searches and follow-up questions it needs can't be known until it sees intermediate results. What fits best?",o:["An agent that decides its own next step in a loop based on what it finds","A workflow with exactly one hard-coded branch point","A fixed three-step workflow written in advance","A single synchronous call with no tool access"],c:[0],e:"When the path genuinely depends on results you can't predict beforehand, only a model deciding step-by-step can adapt."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Compared with a workflow, what do you generally give up by choosing an agent?",o:["Any meaningful control over cost, since agents ignore token budgets entirely","The ability to call tools during execution","Predictability and ease of testing, because the exact sequence of steps can vary between runs","Compatibility with the underlying Messages API"],c:[2],e:"Agents trade a fixed, testable path for the flexibility to adapt — that's the core cost of choosing dynamic control flow."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A team builds a fully autonomous agent for a task that, on reflection, only ever follows one of two known paths depending on a single upfront classification. What's the likely consequence?",o:["The agent will be unable to call any tools at all","The task becomes impossible to complete with an LLM","Costs become fixed and unrelated to how many calls are made","Unnecessary loss of testability and predictability"],c:[3],e:"If the branching is actually bounded and known, an agent adds complexity without buying anything a workflow with one decision point couldn't already do."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A coordinator agent hands a market-research subtask to one subagent and a technical-feasibility subtask to another, then combines both write-ups into one report. What pattern is this?",o:["A Message Batches API job split across two custom IDs","A single linear workflow with no branching","Prompt caching applied across two separate requests","A manager/subagent (orchestrator-workers) pattern"],c:[3],e:"A lead agent delegating distinct subtasks to specialized subagents and integrating their results is the orchestrator-workers pattern."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Delegating work to specialized subagents pays off most clearly when subtasks are:",o:["Trivial enough that a single prompt already handles them well","Impossible to divide without losing essential shared context","Separable by domain or independent enough to run in parallel","Guaranteed to require exactly one tool call each"],c:[2],e:"Orchestration earns its overhead when work can genuinely be split — by expertise, by independence, or both."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Why might a manager agent deliberately isolate each subagent's working context from its own?",o:["Because subagents are technically incapable of using tools","Because it permanently disables the manager from ever reading subagent output","Because isolation is required before any API call can succeed","So a subagent's exploratory dead ends don't dilute the manager's own context"],c:[3],e:"Isolation exists to protect the manager's context from a subtask's noisy exploration — the subagent still reports back a result."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A manager agent gives every subagent read access to the manager's entire running context, including unrelated prior subtasks. What's the most likely downside?",o:["Subagents becoming unable to return results to the manager","Context bloat and cross-task interference from irrelevant carried-over history","Automatic disabling of the manager's own tool access","Faster responses across the board, since more context always helps"],c:[1],e:"Sharing everything defeats the purpose of scoping a subagent's context to just what its subtask needs."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"What does the Claude Agent SDK add on top of calling the Messages API directly yourself?",o:["A guarantee that no further prompt engineering is required","Mandatory hosting of your application on Anthropic's infrastructure","Scaffolding for the agent loop itself — tool execution, context, and turn-taking","A replacement for the Messages API that uses a different underlying model"],c:[2],e:"The SDK sits on top of the same API, providing the loop mechanics rather than replacing the API or the need for good prompts."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A team building a custom agent loop by hand — parsing stop reasons, dispatching tool calls, reassembling context — is essentially reimplementing:",o:["A capability that doesn't exist anywhere in Anthropic's tooling","What the Claude Agent SDK already provides as scaffolding","The Message Batches API's internal request queue","MCP's resource-listing protocol"],c:[1],e:"That loop-management logic is precisely what the Agent SDK is meant to save you from writing yourself."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"In an agent loop, a 'hook' is best described as:",o:["A point in the loop's lifecycle where you can intercept or block an action","A parameter that selects which model tier handles the request","A caching mechanism that stores repeated prompt prefixes","A UI widget for displaying streamed tokens"],c:[0],e:"Hooks are lifecycle interception points, distinct from caching, model selection, or UI concerns."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A hook is configured to require confirmation before any tool call that deletes data can execute. What is this hook doing?",o:["Caching the deletion request so it can be replayed later","Logging the deletion after it has already completed, for audit purposes only","Gating a specific class of destructive action at a defined lifecycle point before it runs","Selecting a cheaper model tier for delete operations specifically"],c:[2],e:"Blocking before execution is a pre-action gate, not a post-hoc log or an unrelated optimization."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A separate hook records every tool call an agent makes, with no ability to block anything. What purpose does this hook serve?",o:["Determining whether the task is better suited to a workflow or an agent","Selecting which model tier is used for each call","Observability — an audit trail of what the agent did, without changing outcomes","The exact same purpose as a hook that blocks destructive actions before they run"],c:[2],e:"A passive logging hook and an active blocking hook serve different purposes even though both are lifecycle interceptions."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Which best summarizes the basic loop most tool-using agent implementations follow?",o:["Send a request, check why it stopped, execute any requested tool, and repeat until done","Send one request, return whatever text comes back, and never inspect why generation stopped","Stream partial tokens to the user with tool calls disabled for the whole session","Batch every request overnight regardless of how urgent the task is"],c:[0],e:"The defining shape of an agent loop is checking the stop reason and looping on tool execution until the model is actually done."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Why should a production agent loop cap the number of tool-call iterations or set an overall timeout?",o:["Bounding iterations disables the model's ability to call more than one tool ever","Timeouts are the only way to enable tool use in the first place","Iteration caps are required before the Messages API will accept any request","Without a bound, a misbehaving loop can keep calling tools indefinitely"],c:[3],e:"A cap is a safety net against runaway loops, not a prerequisite for tool use itself."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A graph-based orchestration library (in the style of LangGraph) is most useful compared to a plain linear workflow when:",o:["The task has genuine branches or loops a straight-line sequence can't cleanly express","You specifically want to avoid making any LLM calls","The application has no need for state to persist between steps","The task is a strictly linear sequence with no conditional logic anywhere"],c:[0],e:"Graph orchestration earns its added complexity once you need conditional branching or looping beyond a simple line of steps."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"How should a team building on Claude generally think about third-party orchestration frameworks like LangGraph?",o:["As a mandatory dependency that every Claude application must include","As an optional layer for structuring multi-step coordination","As a type of MCP server that ships tools and resources","As a direct, drop-in replacement for the Messages API itself"],c:[1],e:"These frameworks are optional coordination layers built on top of the API, not a requirement or a protocol like MCP."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A support-triage system sometimes needs to answer directly, sometimes escalate to a human, and sometimes call a refund tool — and which path is right isn't knowable until the ticket is read. This case favors:",o:["A fixed workflow that always escalates first regardless of the ticket","An agent that can choose among those paths per ticket","Processing every ticket through the Message Batches API exclusively","Disabling tool use so the model can only produce text"],c:[1],e:"Per-instance unpredictability in which path is correct is a defining case for letting the model decide rather than hard-coding a path."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A workflow contains four fixed steps, but step 3 branches to one of two fixed sub-paths based on a classification made in step 2. Is this still reasonably called a workflow?",o:["No — any branching at all automatically makes it an agent by definition","Yes — a small, bounded set of branches doesn't turn a workflow into a full agent","Yes, but only if the branching step also calls an external tool","No — workflows can never contain more than one step total"],c:[1],e:"A workflow can include a small number of known, bounded branch points and still be far more predictable and testable than an open-ended agent."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Why is an agent's behavior generally harder to write deterministic tests against than a workflow's?",o:["Agents cannot produce any output that a test could inspect","Testing frameworks are structurally incompatible with any LLM-based system","Workflows are incapable of producing testable output at all","The path the agent takes can differ between runs based on the model's own decisions"],c:[3],e:"Run-to-run variability in the path taken is what makes exact-match, deterministic assertions harder for agents than for workflows."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"All else equal, how does an agent's cost typically compare to a workflow's for the same underlying task?",o:["Workflows never incur any API cost under any circumstances","Agent cost is fixed in advance regardless of how many tool calls happen","It depends mainly on how many LLM calls actually get made, not the architecture label","Agents are inherently and unconditionally more expensive purely by virtue of being agents"],c:[2],e:"Cost tracks actual usage — call count and size — not whether you've labeled the thing a workflow or an agent."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A subagent in an orchestrator pattern typically operates:",o:["With unrestricted visibility into every other subagent's full history by default","Scoped to the specific piece of work it was delegated, often with its own isolated working context","Only in place of tool use, never alongside it","Without any way to return a result back to the manager"],c:[1],e:"Subagents are scoped delegates — bounded to their subtask and typically isolated, not given free rein over everything else."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"An orchestrator dispatches three subagents to work in parallel, but the manager has no defined strategy for merging their three outputs into one answer. What's missing?",o:["A fixed workflow, which is required before any subagent can run at all","Nothing — parallel subagents automatically merge their own outputs with no coordination needed","A result-aggregation step — delegation alone doesn't produce a coherent final answer","Tool access for the subagents, which is unrelated to how their outputs get combined"],c:[2],e:"Orchestration needs both delegation and a deliberate integration step; skipping the second leaves you with fragments, not an answer."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"When is a manager/subagent hierarchy probably overkill for a task?",o:["Whenever the task involves any external data retrieval at all","When the task is small enough for one agent to handle directly","Whenever more than one tool is available to the agent","Whenever the response needs to be streamed to a user interface"],c:[1],e:"Orchestration overhead is only worth paying when there's real work to split; a small, undivided task doesn't need a manager layer."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"What should primarily drive the choice between building a workflow and building an agent for a given task?",o:["Always defaulting to a workflow, since predictability is inherently better in every case","Whichever architecture is easier to demo to stakeholders, regardless of task fit","Always defaulting to an agent, since more autonomy is inherently better in every case","How predictable and bounded the actual task is, not a blanket preference for one architecture over the other"],c:[3],e:"Matching architecture to task shape — not a fixed default in either direction — is the sound approach."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A subagent finishes a delegated research task and needs to hand its findings back to the manager agent. How does this typically happen?",o:["The subagent returns a result that the manager folds into its own context","Results can only be delivered by disabling context isolation beforehand","The subagent must call the Message Batches API to deliver its result","The subagent's entire isolated context automatically merges into the manager's context afterward"],c:[0],e:"The point of isolation is that only the useful result crosses back, not the subagent's full exploratory context."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Sequential subagent delegation (each subagent starts only after the previous one finishes) differs from parallel delegation mainly in that sequential delegation:",o:["Always costs less than parallel delegation regardless of task shape","Is never a valid pattern in an orchestrator design","Eliminates the need for any result aggregation afterward","Trades speed for letting each later subagent build on earlier results"],c:[3],e:"Sequential delegation is slower than parallel but lets later steps depend on earlier results — a real, task-dependent tradeoff."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A vague, one-line tool description on a subagent's only available tool causes the subagent to call it incorrectly. Whose responsibility is that, architecturally?",o:["The tool's design — poor descriptions degrade tool-selection accuracy regardless of caller","It can only happen inside a manager/subagent hierarchy, never in a single-agent setup","It's unrelated to tool design and is purely a model-tier problem","It only happens with server-side tools, never client-side ones"],c:[0],e:"Tool-description quality affects correct invocation everywhere tools are used, not just in orchestrated multi-agent setups."},
{d:"D1",t:"m",q:"Which two situations most clearly favor a fixed, code-orchestrated workflow over an agent? (Select 2)",o:["Deterministic, repeatable testing of the whole sequence is a priority","The exact steps and their order are already known and repeat identically each run","Maximum adaptability to genuinely unanticipated situations is the primary goal","The number of steps needed depends on results that are only known once the task starts"],c:[0,1],e:"Known, repeatable steps and a need for deterministic testing both point toward a workflow; the other two are classic agent triggers."},
{d:"D1",t:"m",q:"Which two are legitimate reasons to use a manager/subagent orchestration pattern rather than a single agent? (Select 2)",o:["Cost needs to be minimized regardless of whether delegation actually helps","Subtasks require distinct domain expertise that's easier to isolate per subagent","Subtasks can run independently of one another and benefit from parallel execution","The task has exactly one step and no meaningful subdivision is possible"],c:[1,2],e:"Orchestration pays off when subtasks are genuinely separable by expertise or parallelizable — not when there's nothing to divide."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"In a Messages API request, where do system-level instructions belong?",o:["Appended to the end of the last user message before sending","Inside the `tools` array alongside the tool definitions","As the first message in the array with role set to 'system'","In a separate top-level `system` parameter"],c:[3],e:"The Messages API treats system instructions as their own top-level field, distinct from the alternating user/assistant messages array."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A Messages API conversation with multiple turns is structured as:",o:["An array alternating user and assistant roles, with `system` set separately as its own parameter","A request where `system` is required to be repeated inside every message","A sequence where any number of consecutive messages can share the 'assistant' role","A single message containing the entire conversation concatenated as one string"],c:[0],e:"The standard shape is alternating user/assistant turns, with system instructions kept outside that alternating array."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"What must every Messages API request supply at minimum to be valid?",o:["A `tools` array and an explicit `system` prompt","A model identifier, a `messages` array, and a `max_tokens` value","At least one image content block, even for text-only tasks","A cache breakpoint and a Batch API job ID"],c:[1],e:"Those three fields are the baseline every request needs; tools, system, and caching are all optional additions."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"After the model returns a `tool_use` content block, what does the calling application need to do before the conversation can continue?",o:["Switch the request into streaming mode before anything else can happen","Execute the requested tool and send its result back as a `tool_result` block in a subsequent request","Immediately show the raw `tool_use` block to the end user as the final answer","Discard the turn and restart the conversation from the system prompt"],c:[1],e:"Tool use is a round trip: the app runs the tool, then reports the outcome back so the model can continue."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A `tool_result` block is linked back to the model's request by:",o:["Referencing the `id` of the specific `tool_use` block it is answering","Including a hash of the entire prior conversation history","Being sent within the same streaming event as the original tool_use block","Matching on the exact text of the tool's name alone, with no id involved"],c:[0],e:"The `id` field is what ties a tool_use request to its corresponding tool_result response."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"The Messages API is stateless between calls. What does this mean for a multi-turn agentic loop?",o:["Your application must resend the relevant prior turns on each subsequent call","Statelessness only affects vision requests, not text or tool-use turns","Nothing needs to be resent, since the API automatically retains full history server-side by default","Only the original system prompt needs to be resent, with all message history dropped"],c:[0],e:"Because there's no server-side session state by default, the calling application owns reconstructing the relevant history each call."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A chat UI needs generated text to visibly appear word-by-word as it's produced. Which mechanism enables this?",o:["Streaming, which delivers the response as a sequence of incremental events","The Message Batches API, which is asynchronous and not meant for live UI updates","Prompt caching, which only affects repeated prefixes, not delivery timing","Vision content blocks, which are unrelated to how text is delivered"],c:[0],e:"Streaming is specifically the mechanism for incremental delivery of output as it's generated."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A `content_block_delta` event received during a streamed response represents:",o:["An incremental piece of the response as it's generated","A tool_result being returned to the model","A notification that the prompt cache was invalidated","The entire finished message, sent as one single event"],c:[0],e:"Delta events are the incremental chunks that streaming sends as generation progresses."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"An integration must validate the model's entire JSON response as one complete object before doing anything with it. What does this suggest about streaming?",o:["Streaming likely isn't worth the added complexity here","Streaming is mandatory in this case regardless of the validation requirement","Streaming would make validating the complete object easier, not harder","This scenario is only possible if tool use is disabled entirely"],c:[0],e:"If you need the whole object before acting, incremental delivery doesn't help and just adds reassembly work."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Which content type lets Claude read the contents of a screenshot or photograph provided in a request?",o:["A cache_control block marked as ephemeral","A vision (image) content block within the message","An extended thinking block, which only carries the model's own reasoning text","A `tool_result` block, regardless of what generated the image"],c:[1],e:"Image understanding is handled via image content blocks placed directly in the message content."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A finance workflow needs to read scanned invoice images and return structured JSON totals. Which combination of capabilities is directly relevant?",o:["The Message Batches API specifically, with no need for vision at all","Extended thinking alone, without any image content blocks","Vision content blocks plus a structured-output request with downstream validation","Prompt caching alone, since the invoices are the same length each time"],c:[2],e:"Reading the scanned images requires vision; reliably getting totals out requires requesting and then validating structured output."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"An overnight job must process 50,000 records with no requirement for an immediate response, and cost efficiency matters most. Which approach fits best?",o:["The Message Batches API, which processes large volumes asynchronously at half price","Synchronous calls issued one after another in a tight loop","Extended thinking enabled on every single record regardless of complexity","Streaming every response to a user interface that no one is watching overnight"],c:[0],e:"High volume, latency tolerance, and cost sensitivity together are exactly the Batch API's design target."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A single Message Batch submission is limited to:",o:["100,000 individual requests or 256 MB of total request size, whichever limit is hit first","Unlimited requests, as long as each one finishes within one second","Exactly 1,000 requests, with no size-based limit at all","10 requests per batch, renewable every 5 minutes"],c:[0],e:"Anthropic's Batch API caps a single batch at 100,000 requests or 256 MB, whichever comes first."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"When retrieving results from a completed Message Batch, an application should match each result back to its original request using:",o:["The timestamp on each result, compared against submission timestamps","The order results appear in the results file, which always matches submission order exactly","The model name used, since only one model can appear per batch","The `custom_id` supplied with that request, since results are not guaranteed to come back in submission order"],c:[3],e:"Batch results can return in any order, so `custom_id` — not position — is the reliable way to match them up."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A request sets `stream: true` inside a batch of requests submitted to the Message Batches API. What happens?",o:["It's accepted, and each batched request streams independently in real time","It automatically converts the entire batch into synchronous calls","It's silently ignored with no effect on the request at all","It's rejected — streaming isn't supported for batch requests"],c:[3],e:"Certain parameters, including `stream: true`, are explicitly incompatible with the asynchronous nature of batch processing and return a validation error."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Batch results remain available for download after a batch completes for approximately:",o:["Indefinitely, with no expiration on stored results","29 days from batch creation, after which results are no longer downloadable","1 hour, matching the typical processing window","24 hours only, after which the batch itself is deleted entirely"],c:[1],e:"Batch results stay downloadable for 29 days after creation; the processing window itself is a separate, shorter figure."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A system prompt is long and unchanging, while each request appends a short, unique user question. What reduces cost and latency here?",o:["Routing every one of these latency-sensitive calls through the Batch API","Shortening the unique question instead, since it's the smaller part of the prompt","Marking the stable system prompt as cacheable so its tokens get reused, not reprocessed","Switching to the cheapest available model tier regardless of task difficulty"],c:[2],e:"A large, stable, repeated prefix is exactly the case prompt caching is built to optimize."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"By default, how long does a prompt cache entry remain valid if it isn't reused?",o:["5 minutes, measured from the start of the request, refreshed on each reuse","Indefinitely, until the underlying model version changes","24 hours by default, matching the Batch API's processing window","30 seconds, with no way to extend it under any circumstance"],c:[0],e:"The default cache lifetime is 5 minutes from the start of the triggering request, refreshed on each subsequent hit."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A background agent session sometimes goes longer than 5 minutes between calls that share a cached prefix, and the team wants to avoid repeated cache-write costs. What's the direct fix?",o:["Switch the session to the Batch API, which doesn't support prompt caching at all","Reduce max_tokens to zero so no cache write is ever triggered","Disable caching entirely for that session, since caching is now counterproductive","Use the 1-hour cache duration option instead of the 5-minute default, at its higher per-write price"],c:[3],e:"The 1-hour TTL exists specifically for reuse gaps too long for the 5-minute default but still valuable to keep warm."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Prompt caching treats a request's content in which hierarchy?",o:["System only — tools and messages are never part of the cached prefix","Messages, then system, then tools, with no dependency between levels","A single flat list with no meaningful ordering between sections","Tools, then system, then messages, with earlier changes invalidating what follows"],c:[3],e:"Caching follows a tools → system → messages hierarchy, and a change at one level invalidates it and every level that follows."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"An application can define at most how many explicit cache breakpoints in a single request?",o:["4","10, matching the maximum number of tool definitions allowed","1, since only the very last block can ever be marked cacheable","Unlimited, since every content block can carry its own breakpoint"],c:[0],e:"Explicit cache_control breakpoints are capped at 4 per request; automatic caching uses one of those slots."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A team caches a system prompt that's only 300 tokens long and never sees a cache hit register in the usage fields. What's the most likely explanation?",o:["The prompt falls below the minimum cacheable token threshold for that model","Short prompts always error out instead of being processed at all","The `cache_control` field only applies to image content, never to text","Caching silently caps out at exactly 500 tokens for every model with no exceptions"],c:[0],e:"Minimum cacheable lengths vary by model, and prompts under that threshold are simply processed uncached with no error."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A large, reused reference document is cached, and the same request is also submitted through the Message Batches API for cost reasons. Is this combination valid?",o:["Yes — caching and batching address different cost levers and can be combined","No — enabling the Batch API automatically strips any cache_control fields from the request","No — caching and batching are mutually exclusive features that cannot appear together","Yes, but only if vision content is also present in the request"],c:[0],e:"The two mechanisms optimize different things and are designed to stack, including inside batch requests."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A team adds per-request dynamic effort tuning to an agent loop and notices their message-level cache hit rate drops sharply. Why?",o:["Changing the effort value between requests invalidates the message-level cache","Cache hit rate dropped because vision was silently enabled","Effort settings have no relationship to caching behavior at all","Effort changes only affect the Batch API, never synchronous caching"],c:[0],e:"Because effort is rendered into the prompt, changing it between requests breaks the cached prefix at the message level."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A production incident traces back to a model alias silently changing behavior after Anthropic shipped an update. What practice would have prevented this?",o:["Enabling extended thinking on every request","Switching every call to go through the Batch API","Pinning the integration to a dated model snapshot instead of a rolling alias","Increasing max_tokens across the board"],c:[2],e:"Dated, pinned snapshots don't shift behavior the way an alias pointing at 'whatever's current' can."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"CLAUDE.md is best described as:",o:["A required file for API authentication with no other purpose","A file that exclusively defines which MCP servers are available","A raw log of past conversations kept for audit purposes","A project-level file providing Claude with standing context about the project"],c:[3],e:"CLAUDE.md exists to give Claude durable, project-specific context, not to handle auth, logging, or MCP configuration alone."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Nesting CLAUDE.md files in subdirectories of a project primarily enables:",o:["Automatic pinning of the model version used in that subdirectory","Disabling settings.json for that part of the project","More specific guidance for a subdirectory that layers on top of the root-level file","Faster raw API response times for requests touching that subdirectory"],c:[2],e:"Nested files let context get more specific the deeper you go, cascading on top of the broader root guidance."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"In a Claude Code project, `settings.json` is primarily where you configure:",o:["The model's underlying training weights","Tool permissions, hooks, and related environment configuration","The schema of a connected vector database","Billing thresholds for the Anthropic account"],c:[1],e:"settings.json governs permissions, hooks, and similar environment configuration, not model internals or billing."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A tool's `input_schema` (JSON Schema) exists to:",o:["Authenticate the request against the Anthropic API","Style how the tool's results are displayed in a chat interface","Control which prompt-cache breakpoint the tool's definition uses","Define the structure and types of arguments the model should supply"],c:[3],e:"The schema shapes what arguments the model is expected to produce for a given tool call."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A tool description reads only 'does math stuff' with no further detail, while the tool actually performs currency conversion specifically. What's the likely consequence?",o:["No effect at all, since tool descriptions are cosmetic and don't influence model behavior","Inconsistent or incorrect invocation, since the model has little to go on","Guaranteed correct use, since any description at all satisfies the schema requirement","Automatic correction of the mismatch by the API before the request is sent"],c:[1],e:"Vague, mismatched descriptions directly degrade the model's ability to select and use a tool appropriately."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A feature parses the model's text output as JSON downstream. What should the integration code do regardless of how the JSON was requested?",o:["Disable all error handling around parsing to reduce latency","Defensively validate and handle malformed or unexpected output","Only accept the output if it also contains a vision content block","Trust that the output will always match the expected schema exactly"],c:[1],e:"Even with a structured-output request, downstream code should still validate rather than assume perfect compliance."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"What does `max_tokens` govern in a Messages API request?",o:["How long a prompt-cache entry remains valid","The maximum number of input tokens the request is allowed to contain","The number of tool calls the model is permitted to make in one turn","The maximum number of tokens the model is allowed to generate in its response"],c:[3],e:"max_tokens caps generated output length; it doesn't bound the input, cache TTL, or tool-call count directly."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A feature sets `max_tokens` far higher than the task ever actually needs. What's the main practical downside?",o:["The request will always fail validation outright","Tool use is silently disabled for the rest of the conversation","Vision content blocks become unusable for that request","Unnecessary cost and latency exposure from overly long generations"],c:[3],e:"An overly generous max_tokens doesn't break the request, but it removes a natural guardrail against unnecessarily long, costly generations."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Why is exact-match string comparison a poor default assertion for testing an LLM feature's output?",o:["Because the API guarantees byte-identical output on every call, making the assertion redundant","Because exact-match assertions only ever fail on vision-based tasks","Because LLM output can never be tested by any automated method","Sampling-driven non-determinism means identical prompts can validly produce different wordings"],c:[3],e:"Expected output variability makes exact-match a fragile choice; tests should target acceptable properties instead."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A CI pipeline for an LLM feature runs only traditional unit tests on the surrounding code. What's missing?",o:["Automated eval checks assessing the quality and correctness of the model's actual outputs","Nothing — traditional unit tests are sufficient for any LLM-integrated feature","A mandatory switch to the Batch API before any test can run","A requirement to disable non-determinism before merging"],c:[0],e:"LLM output quality needs its own eval-style checks in CI, layered on top of ordinary unit testing of the surrounding code."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A code review finds a mobile app's API key embedded directly in the client-side bundle. This is primarily a failure of:",o:["Secure secret management — client-side code isn't a safe place for API credentials","Model version pinning discipline, which concerns behavior stability, not secrets","Streaming protocol conventions, which don't govern secret handling","Prompt caching configuration, which is unrelated to how secrets are stored"],c:[0],e:"Hardcoded client-side secrets are a textbook secret-management failure, independent of any LLM-specific concern."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Code review for an LLM-integration pull request should specifically add checks for, beyond ordinary code quality:",o:["Whether extended thinking is enabled for every single request","Only font choices and visual styling in any related UI code","Whether the code avoids using the Messages API entirely","Exposure to prompt injection, error handling around tool calls, and secret management"],c:[3],e:"LLM-specific risks — injection exposure, tool-call error handling, secrets — need deliberate review attention beyond generic code quality."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Refactoring a tangled prompt-assembly function into smaller, named pieces primarily improves:",o:["Automatic enablement of prompt caching with no other changes needed","Maintainability and testability as the surrounding requirements continue to evolve","Guaranteed lower token costs on every subsequent call","The model's inherent reasoning capability on the resulting prompts"],c:[1],e:"Refactoring is a code-health investment; it doesn't directly change model behavior or automatically trigger caching."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Which reflects sound REST API design applied to the service layer wrapping a Claude integration?",o:["Randomizing response shapes between calls to discourage client-side caching","Endpoint names copied directly from internal database table names with no consistency","Resource-oriented endpoints, predictable status codes, and a clear versioning strategy","Avoiding the use of standard HTTP status codes wherever possible"],c:[2],e:"Standard REST discipline — clear resources, predictable status codes, versioning — still applies to the layer around your Claude calls."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Why are async/await patterns generally recommended for LLM API calls inside a concurrent web backend?",o:["Because async is required before vision content blocks can be used","Because LLM calls are CPU-bound and async is the only way to use multiple cores","LLM calls are I/O-bound, and blocking synchronously on them wastes resources under load","Because it removes the need for any error handling around the call"],c:[2],e:"The benefit is specifically about not blocking on network I/O while waiting for a response, which matters most under concurrency."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A stakeholder asks for an LLM feature that will 'make our support better,' with nothing more specific. What should happen before implementation choices are made?",o:["The vague goal should be decomposed into concrete, testable use cases and success criteria","Implementation should start immediately with the most capable available model tier","A CLAUDE.md file should be written before anything else is discussed","The team should skip requirements work entirely and iterate purely in production"],c:[0],e:"Turning ambiguous asks into testable criteria is foundational groundwork that should precede technical choices like model tier."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Why does requirements gathering for an LLM feature often take more deliberate effort than for traditional deterministic software?",o:["Because LLM output is always identical regardless of the prompt, removing the need for criteria","Because LLM features never need any requirements gathering at all","Because deterministic software never has ambiguous requirements either","Success criteria for natural-language, probabilistic output are often fuzzier to pin down than for a deterministic spec"],c:[3],e:"Defining what 'good' output looks like is genuinely harder when output is probabilistic and open-ended by nature."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"An iterative build-measure-learn cycle applied to an LLM feature typically starts with:",o:["Skipping measurement entirely to move faster toward a final release","Shipping a minimal version and observing real failure patterns first","A single, fully polished release with no further changes planned afterward","Finalizing model tier only after every other design decision is locked in"],c:[1],e:"Starting minimal and learning from real observed failures is the core of the iterative loop, rather than over-building upfront."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Which practice most directly reduces the impact of a bad model response actually reaching a user?",o:["Trusting model output without validation, to minimize added latency","Disabling logging so issues are less visible to the team","Removing error handling from the tool-call path to simplify the code","Validating or guarding the output before it's shown or acted on"],c:[3],e:"A validation/guard step is the barrier between generation and user-facing impact — removing it increases exposure, not reduces it."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A high-traffic, cost-sensitive customer-facing feature needs predictable spend at scale. Which combination best addresses this?",o:["Disabling streaming entirely, regardless of whether the feature has a live UI","Vision content blocks added to every request regardless of whether images are involved","The most capable tier for every call, with caching and batching both disabled","A right-sized model tier, prompt caching for shared context, and batching for non-real-time work"],c:[3],e:"Layering tier-matching, caching, and batching together is the standard cost-optimization stack for high-traffic features."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A product requirement demands a single response within 300ms. Which mechanism is ruled out by that constraint?",o:["Synchronous calls using a fast model tier, which can plausibly meet tight latency budgets","Streaming, which is specifically designed to reduce perceived latency","The Message Batches API, since it processes asynchronously and cannot guarantee an immediate response","Tool use in general, which has no inherent effect on request latency by itself"],c:[2],e:"Batch processing is fundamentally asynchronous and unsuited to a hard, immediate single-response latency requirement."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Anthropic's Files API is most useful for:",o:["Storing API keys outside of environment variables","Automatically pinning a model version for every request","Uploading a file once and referencing it across multiple subsequent requests","Replacing the need for the `messages` array entirely"],c:[2],e:"The Files API lets you upload content once and reuse a reference to it, avoiding repeated re-transmission of the same file."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A RAG application wants each generated claim to point back to the specific source passage it came from. Which capability is designed for this?",o:["The Message Batches API, which affects processing mode, not sourcing","Citations, which attach source attribution to generated content","Prompt caching, which affects cost and latency but not attribution","Extended thinking, which exposes reasoning but not source attribution"],c:[1],e:"Citations are the feature purpose-built for natural, per-claim source attribution in retrieval-style applications."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Structured output requests (asking the model to conform to a defined output format) reduce, but don't eliminate, the need for:",o:["Defensive parsing and validation of the response actually received","Prompt caching, which structured output requests are incompatible with","Tool use, which is unrelated to structured output requests","Model selection, which structured output requests replace entirely"],c:[0],e:"Requesting structure improves the odds of compliant output, but the integration should still verify what actually came back."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"`tool_choice` in a Messages API request controls:",o:["Whether and how the model is permitted or required to use tools on that turn","How long the prompt cache entry for that request remains valid","Which model tier processes the request","Whether the response is streamed or returned synchronously"],c:[0],e:"tool_choice governs the model's tool-use behavior for the turn, separate from model tier, streaming, or caching."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A single model turn returns multiple `tool_use` blocks requesting several different tool calls at once. This is best described as:",o:["An error state that should never occur and always be treated as invalid","Behavior that only server-side tools are capable of triggering","A sign that streaming must be disabled for the rest of the session","Parallel tool use, where the model requests more than one tool call within a single turn"],c:[3],e:"Requesting multiple tool calls in one turn is a supported, named pattern rather than an error condition."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Client-side tools are executed:",o:["Within your own application or environment, not on Anthropic's infrastructure","Entirely on Anthropic's servers, identical to server-side tools","Only inside a browser, never in a backend service","Only when the request is submitted through the Message Batches API"],c:[0],e:"Client-side tools run in the calling application's own environment — that's the defining difference from server-side tools."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Deciding whether a capability should be a client-side tool or a server-side tool mainly comes down to:",o:["Whether the tool's name is longer than a fixed character limit","Whether the request also happens to include an image content block","Whether the conversation is being streamed to a UI","Where the execution needs to happen and who's responsible for running the underlying logic"],c:[3],e:"The client-vs-server distinction is fundamentally about execution location and ownership of the underlying logic."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"The extended/adaptive thinking content block, when present in a response, is exposed:",o:["As its own distinct content block, separate from the final answer text","Only when the response is delivered synchronously rather than streamed","Only via a completely separate API endpoint requiring a second request","Never — it is fully hidden from every API response by design"],c:[0],e:"Thinking content comes back as its own block type alongside, but distinct from, the final answer."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A tool call to an external service times out mid-conversation. What should a well-designed integration do?",o:["Silently return an empty response with no indication anything went wrong","Terminate the entire application process immediately on any tool failure","Implement explicit error handling so the failure is surfaced and handled","Permanently disable that tool for all future sessions after one timeout"],c:[2],e:"A handled, visible failure path is the correct response to a tool error — not silence or a disproportionate shutdown."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A response comes back with `stop_reason: \"pause_turn\"` after a server tool (like web search) was used mid-turn. What does this indicate?",o:["The request failed outright and must be resubmitted from scratch","Vision content was detected and rejected by the server tool","The turn hasn't finished — continue it with the paused content in a follow-up request","The conversation has permanently ended and no further turns are possible"],c:[2],e:"`pause_turn` signals an in-progress server-side turn that needs to be continued, not a failure or a hard stop."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Reviewing the `stop_reason` field on a response is useful because:",o:["It is only populated when the request used vision content","It only ever has one possible value across all requests","It reveals the specific reason generation stopped, which should drive different handling","It replaces the need to check whether a tool_use block is present"],c:[2],e:"Different stop reasons call for different follow-up logic, so checking this field is a core part of building a correct integration."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A team notices their prompt-cache read tokens don't count against their organization's rate limit the way uncached input tokens do. What's the practical implication?",o:["Rate limits become entirely irrelevant once any caching is enabled","This behavior only applies to requests submitted via the Batch API","A high cache-hit rate can meaningfully improve effective rate-limit headroom","Cache reads instead count double against the rate limit as a tradeoff"],c:[2],e:"Because cache hits aren't deducted from rate limits, effective caching helps with both cost and available request throughput."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A regulated industry's compliance team asks how request and response content is retained by default. What's the relevant integration consideration?",o:["Nothing — no Claude API feature has any data retention behavior worth reviewing","Understanding the platform's data retention and ZDR options, since eligibility varies by feature","ZDR eligibility is identical and automatic across every single API feature with no review needed","Retention only matters for requests that use vision content blocks"],c:[1],e:"Retention and ZDR eligibility genuinely differ by feature, so a compliance-sensitive integration needs to actually check, not assume."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A team deciding between a conversational chat UI and a fully autonomous backend agent for the same task should weigh primarily:",o:["How much visibility and control the situation actually calls for","The model tier chosen, which should be decided independently of interface design","Whether tools are involved at all, since that alone should decide the interface","Which option is cheaper to build regardless of how well it fits the task"],c:[0],e:"Interface choice should follow the task's actual need for human visibility and control, not be decided on unrelated grounds."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A fully autonomous backend agent with no human review step is most defensible when:",o:["The task is high-risk and difficult to verify once it's already been taken","The task is well-bounded, relatively low-risk, and its outcomes are easy to verify after the fact","The budget for the project happens to be unusually large","Users have explicitly asked for maximum visibility into every step taken"],c:[1],e:"Full autonomy fits situations where mistakes are cheap to catch and correct, not high-stakes or hard-to-verify ones."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Version control hygiene for an LLM-integrated codebase should include:",o:["Small, reviewable commits alongside pinned model and dependency versions","Skipping code review to move through changes faster","API keys committed directly to the repository for convenience","One large commit at the end of each development cycle"],c:[0],e:"Small commits and pinned versions support traceability and stable, reviewable behavior over time."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A tool that performs an irreversible action, like permanently deleting a customer record, is called directly from a backend integration with no additional step. What integration-level gap does this reflect?",o:["A missing vision content block, unrelated to how the deletion is triggered","A missing prompt-cache breakpoint on the delete tool's definition","Missing a confirmation or verification gate before an irreversible action is allowed to execute","A missing Batch API submission for the delete request"],c:[2],e:"Irreversible actions generally warrant an added confirmation step in the integration layer before they're allowed to run."},
{d:"D2",t:"m",q:"Which two are parameters or features explicitly unsupported inside a Message Batches API request? (Select 2)",o:["System prompts via the top-level `system` parameter","`stream: true`","Fast mode's `speed` parameter","Tool definitions in the `tools` array"],c:[1,2],e:"Streaming and fast mode's speed setting are both explicitly incompatible with asynchronous batch processing; tools and system prompts work fine in batches."},
{d:"D2",t:"m",q:"Which three of these are true about combining prompt caching with the Message Batches API on the same workload? (Select 3)",o:["Cost efficiency matters more than immediate turnaround for this workload","A large, stable prefix reused across many of the batched requests supports caching","Cache hits inside a batch are provided on a best-effort basis, not a guarantee","The workload requires a guaranteed sub-second response for each individual request"],c:[0,1,2],e:"A shared stable prefix, a cost-over-latency priority, and best-effort (not guaranteed) cache hits are all accurate for batched caching; a hard sub-second guarantee conflicts with how batching works."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A feature classifies thousands of short product reviews per minute as positive or negative, with no need for nuanced judgment. Which model choice fits best?",o:["Whichever model was released most recently, independent of fit","Whichever model has the largest context window, regardless of task needs","The fastest, cheapest tier suited to simple, high-volume classification, such as Claude Haiku 4.5","The most capable tier available, regardless of the task's actual simplicity"],c:[2],e:"Simple, high-volume classification is the textbook case for the fastest, cheapest tier rather than over-provisioning capability."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A legal team needs deep, ambiguous contract-risk analysis where getting the judgment right matters far more than speed or cost. Which choice fits best?",o:["The most capable tier suited to hard reasoning, such as Claude Opus 5 or Claude Fable 5","All tiers perform identically on ambiguous reasoning tasks, so tier choice doesn't matter","Whichever model has the smallest context window","The fastest, cheapest tier available, regardless of the task's difficulty"],c:[0],e:"High-stakes, ambiguous reasoning is exactly where paying for the most capable tier is justified."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A token, in the context of how Claude processes text, is best understood as:",o:["One entire API request, regardless of its length","One tool call made during a turn","A sub-word unit of text used for model processing","Exactly one character of input or output, with no exceptions"],c:[2],e:"Tokens are sub-word units; they don't map cleanly onto a fixed character or word count."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A model's context window represents:",o:["The number of tool definitions permitted in a single request","Only the number of tokens the model may generate in its response","The combined input-plus-output token budget available for a single request","Only the input tokens a request may contain, with output governed separately"],c:[2],e:"The context window is a shared ceiling spanning both what goes into and comes out of one request."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Why can two calls sent with identical prompts return meaningfully different wording in their responses?",o:["Vision inputs are randomized between identical calls by design","Prompt caching always changes the output whenever it's active","Sampling-driven variability is an inherent, expected part of how generation works","This indicates a bug that should be reported for every occurrence"],c:[2],e:"Non-determinism from sampling is expected behavior, distinct from caching or vision-related effects."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Claude Haiku 4.5 differs from Claude Fable 5, Claude Opus 5, and Claude Sonnet 5 in its thinking behavior in that Haiku 4.5:",o:["Uses adaptive thinking exactly the same way the other three do","Uses a toggleable extended-thinking mode instead of always-adaptive thinking","Always thinks at maximum depth on every request with no way to turn it off","Is the only one of the four with no thinking capability of any kind"],c:[1],e:"Haiku 4.5 supports classic extended thinking as an on/off toggle, while Fable 5, Opus 5, and Sonnet 5 use adaptive thinking that the model itself controls."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"The `effort` parameter is best described as:",o:["A fixed, unchangeable property of a given model that cannot be set per request","A per-request lever trading off response thoroughness against token efficiency","A billing tier selection rather than a behavioral control","A setting that only affects vision-based requests"],c:[1],e:"Effort is a request-level control over token spend across the whole response, tool calls included, not a static model property."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Which named effort level does the API use by default when the `effort` parameter is omitted from a request?",o:["`max`, since the API defaults to unconstrained token spending","`medium`, the balanced default across every supported model","`high` — leaving the parameter unset behaves exactly like setting it explicitly","`low`, chosen to minimize cost unless otherwise specified"],c:[2],e:"The documented default is high effort, and setting effort to \"high\" explicitly changes nothing versus leaving it unset."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Which of these correctly orders the named effort levels from most conservative about token spend to least constrained?",o:["high, medium, xhigh, low, max","max, xhigh, high, medium, low","medium, low, high, max, xhigh","low, medium, high, xhigh, max"],c:[3],e:"The five effort levels run low, medium, high (default), xhigh, then max, from most token-conservative to least constrained."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"The `xhigh` effort level is available on a narrower set of models than `max`. Which of these does NOT support `xhigh`?",o:["Claude Sonnet 5","Claude Opus 4.6","Claude Fable 5","Claude Opus 5"],c:[1],e:"xhigh is supported on Fable 5, Opus 5, Opus 4.8, Opus 4.7, and Sonnet 5, but Opus 4.6 supports only up to max, not xhigh."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Which of Anthropic's current named models does the `effort` parameter NOT apply to?",o:["Claude Opus 5","Claude Haiku 4.5","Claude Fable 5","Claude Sonnet 5"],c:[1],e:"The effort parameter's documented compatibility covers Fable 5, Opus 4.5 and later, and Sonnet 4.6/5 — Haiku 4.5 isn't among the supported models."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Fast mode for Opus is best described as:",o:["A feature available equally across Opus, Sonnet, and Haiku","The same Opus model and quality, running at roughly 2.5x the speed for a higher per-token cost","A free optimization with no cost tradeoff versus standard-speed Opus","An entirely different, smaller model swapped in behind the scenes for speed"],c:[1],e:"Fast mode changes the API configuration for latency, not the model or its quality — and it costs more per token, not less."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Which model family does Fast mode currently support?",o:["Fable only, as the most capable model","Opus only — it is not available on Sonnet, Haiku, or Fable","All current Claude models equally","Sonnet and Haiku, but not Opus"],c:[1],e:"Fast mode is specifically an Opus capability and isn't offered on the other model families."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"How do Fast mode and a lower effort level differ as ways to get a faster response from Opus?",o:["Fast mode reduces quality while lower effort increases cost, the reverse of their actual behavior","They are two names for exactly the same underlying mechanism","Fast mode keeps quality constant and lowers latency for a higher cost; lower effort trades quality for speed","Neither has any measurable effect on response latency"],c:[2],e:"The two levers work differently: one holds quality constant and raises cost for speed, the other trades depth of reasoning for speed and cost savings."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A team wants maximum speed on a straightforward task without needing full reasoning depth. What combination fits Anthropic's documented guidance?",o:["Enable Fast mode together with the max effort level for best results","Disable effort entirely, since Fast mode replaces the need for it","Use the Batch API together with Fast mode for the lowest possible latency","Enable Fast mode together with a lower effort level"],c:[3],e:"Fast mode and a lower effort level are designed to be combined for maximum speed on tasks that don't need deep reasoning."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Why doesn't Fast mode apply to requests submitted through the Message Batches API?",o:["Fast mode requires vision content blocks to be present in the request","The Batch API only supports the cheapest available model tier","Fast mode is too expensive to ever be offered on any high-volume workload","Fast mode optimizes synchronous latency, and batch processing has no live latency to optimize"],c:[3],e:"Fast mode's entire purpose is reducing perceived latency on a live request, which doesn't map onto asynchronous batch processing."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A cost review shows a feature using cache write tokens far more than cache read tokens, at a notably higher per-token price than base input. What does this reflect about caching's pricing structure?",o:["Caching has no effect on per-token pricing in either direction","Cache reads cost more than cache writes, the reverse of their actual pricing","Writing a new cache entry costs more per token than base input; reading one costs far less","Cache writes and cache reads are always priced identically to each other"],c:[2],e:"Cache writes carry a premium over base input price, while cache reads are priced well below it — the pricing intentionally rewards reuse over first-time writes."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Roughly how much cheaper is the Message Batches API compared to standard synchronous pricing, for the same model and volume?",o:["No cheaper at all — batching only affects timing, not price","Batch pricing varies unpredictably with no consistent discount","About 90% cheaper regardless of model","About 50% cheaper across the board"],c:[3],e:"The Batches API applies a flat 50% discount versus standard synchronous pricing for the same model."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Comparing current model context windows, which is true?",o:["Context window size is unrelated to which model you're using","All current models share exactly the same context window size","Claude Haiku 4.5 has the largest context window of the four","Haiku 4.5's context window (200k tokens) is smaller than the others' 1M tokens"],c:[3],e:"Haiku 4.5 tops out at 200k tokens while the other three current-generation models support a 1M-token context window."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Ranking current named models from lowest to highest per-token output price, which order is correct?",o:["Sonnet 5, then Haiku 4.5, then Fable 5, then Opus 5","Fable 5, then Opus 5, then Sonnet 5, then Haiku 4.5","Haiku 4.5, then Sonnet 5, then Opus 5, then Fable 5","All four are priced identically per output token"],c:[2],e:"Pricing scales from Haiku 4.5 as the cheapest tier up through Sonnet 5, Opus 5, and Fable 5 as the most expensive."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A cost-sensitive feature has moderate complexity and needs reasonably fast responses, without an obvious need for the top tier's full capability. What's a sound default starting point?",o:["The most capable tier available, chosen without evaluating whether it's actually needed","A balanced mid-tier model like Claude Sonnet 5, evaluated before assuming a higher tier","A randomly selected tier on each request to average out cost","The cheapest tier available, chosen without evaluating whether it holds quality"],c:[1],e:"A balanced mid-tier model is a reasonable default for moderate-complexity, production-scale work, subject to evaluation."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A cost audit finds the most capable tier being used for simple yes/no classification. What's the likely fix?",o:["Leave it unchanged, since more capability is never a problem for any task","Right-size the tier down to a faster, cheaper model suited to the task's actual complexity","Add a higher effort level on top of the current tier to further improve results","Switch to the Batch API exclusively, without reconsidering the tier itself"],c:[1],e:"Over-provisioned tier for a simple task is fixed by right-sizing down, not by adding more capability or effort on top."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"What's the main risk of defaulting to the cheapest available model tier for every task in an application, regardless of complexity?",o:["Complete loss of API access for the account","No risk at all, since cost is the only factor that ever matters","Degraded quality or accuracy on the subset of tasks that genuinely need deeper reasoning","Guaranteed faster responses on every task with no downside whatsoever"],c:[2],e:"Under-provisioning tier for genuinely hard tasks costs quality, even though it looks efficient for the easy ones."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Why is model tier typically chosen per feature or per task, rather than once for an entire application?",o:["Because tier choice has no measurable effect on output quality","Different features in the same app can have very different complexity and cost needs","Because the same tier is always optimal for every task by definition","Because an API account is only permitted to use one model at a time"],c:[1],e:"Task diversity within one app is exactly why tier decisions are usually made per task rather than app-wide."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A team's evals show a mid-tier model performing inconsistently on a subset of unusually hard cases within an otherwise simple workload. What's a reasonable model-selection response?",o:["Move the entire workload to the most expensive tier to be safe","Route the harder subset to a higher tier, keeping the rest on the cheaper one","Switch the entire workload to the cheapest available tier instead","Ignore the inconsistency, since evals are not meant to inform tier choice"],c:[1],e:"Complexity-based routing lets you preserve quality where it's actually needed without over-paying across the whole workload."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Comparing minimum cacheable prompt lengths, Claude Haiku 4.5 requires a prompt of at least how many tokens before caching applies, versus Claude Opus 5's much lower threshold?",o:["1 token for both, since caching has no minimum length requirement","4,096 tokens for Opus 5, versus 512 tokens for Haiku 4.5, the reverse of the actual thresholds","4,096 tokens for Haiku 4.5, versus 512 tokens for Opus 5","512 tokens for both models, with no difference between them"],c:[2],e:"Minimum cacheable length varies by model, and Haiku 4.5's threshold is notably higher than Opus 5's — a real factor when deciding whether caching will actually help a given Haiku workload."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"When effort is applied to a request that includes tool use, lower effort levels tend to:",o:["Increase the total number of tool calls made compared to higher effort","Have no measurable effect on how tool calls are made","Combine multiple operations into fewer tool calls and proceed with less explanatory preamble","Disable tool use entirely regardless of the tools available"],c:[2],e:"Lower effort tends to economize on tool calls and explanation, while higher effort tends to make more calls with more detailed explanation."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"On Claude Opus 5, what happens if a request sets `thinking: {\"type\": \"disabled\"}` together with `xhigh` or `max` effort?",o:["The request succeeds and effort is automatically downgraded to high instead","The request is rejected with a 400 error, since thinking can't be disabled at those levels","Thinking is disabled but effort is still applied at full strength with no error","The request succeeds, silently ignoring the disabled thinking setting"],c:[1],e:"Opus 5 specifically rejects disabling thinking at xhigh or max effort, a documented hard constraint rather than a silent fallback."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A team is unsure upfront how complex a new feature's real-world inputs will turn out to be. What's a reasonable model-selection strategy?",o:["Randomize the tier used on every request until a pattern emerges","Always start with the most expensive tier available, just to avoid any risk","Refuse to build the feature until complexity is fully known in advance","Start with a mid-tier model and escalate only if evaluation shows it's insufficient"],c:[3],e:"Starting mid-tier and escalating based on evidence avoids both premature over-provisioning and under-provisioning."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Claude Fable 5 and Claude Opus 5 are positioned differently in Anthropic's own guidance. Which best reflects that distinction?",o:["The two models are functionally identical, with no meaningful difference in recommended use case","Opus 5 for agentic coding and enterprise work; Fable 5 for the most demanding, longer-running workloads","Fable 5 is recommended only for simple, low-volume classification tasks","Opus 5 is positioned as strictly more capable than Fable 5 in every respect"],c:[1],e:"Anthropic's own guidance distinguishes Opus 5 as the agentic-coding/enterprise default from Fable 5 as the highest-capability option for the most demanding workloads."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"When comparing two model tiers using an eval suite, why is a single run per tier usually insufficient to draw a confident conclusion?",o:["Because a single run always produces byte-identical results across tiers","Because eval suites are incapable of comparing more than one model at a time","Sampling-driven variability means a single run can look better or worse than typical, purely by chance","Because model comparisons are only valid when performed manually, without eval suites"],c:[2],e:"Given expected run-to-run variability, comparing tiers fairly usually calls for multiple runs rather than trusting a single sample from each."},
{d:"D5",t:"m",q:"Which two factors should primarily drive model tier selection for a given task? (Select 2)",o:["Which model was most recently released, independent of fit","Latency and cost requirements for that specific task","The developer's personal preference for one model's writing style","The task's actual complexity"],c:[1,3],e:"Complexity and latency/cost requirements are the substantive drivers; personal preference and recency aren't sound selection criteria on their own."},
{d:"D5",t:"m",q:"Which three statements about Claude Opus 5's effort levels are accurate? (Select 3)",o:["Opus 5 supports all five named effort levels, from low through max","The documented default effort level on Opus 5 is high","Opus 5 defaults to low effort unless explicitly overridden","Thinking cannot be disabled at xhigh or max effort on Opus 5"],c:[0,1,3],e:"Opus 5 supports the full low-to-max range, defaults to high, and blocks disabling thinking at xhigh/max — it does not default to low."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Over a very long agent session, instructions given at the very start gradually stop being followed even though nothing was explicitly changed. This is best described as:",o:["Context drift — a gradual loss of adherence to earlier instructions","A caching bug that should be reported to the platform","A vision-specific limitation unrelated to text instructions","A tool-use failure caused by a missing tool_result block"],c:[0],e:"Drift refers specifically to this gradual erosion of adherence to earlier instructions over a long session."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A long session accumulates large amounts of low-value exchange history that crowds out genuinely useful context. This is best described as:",o:["Prompt injection, which concerns untrusted content, not accumulated history","Context bloat — irrelevant accumulated history consuming context budget","Context drift, which specifically concerns fading adherence rather than crowding","Model hallucination, which concerns fabricated facts, not context volume"],c:[1],e:"Bloat is about volume and crowding, a distinct problem from drift even though both degrade a long session's quality."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A rule that should hold for an entire conversation — for example, 'always respond in formal English' — belongs in:",o:["The system prompt, since it's a stable, conversation-wide rule","A tool's description field, regardless of whether the rule involves tools","An MCP resource, which is meant for data access, not behavioral rules","The user turn, repeated fresh in every single message"],c:[0],e:"Stable, conversation-wide rules are exactly what the system prompt is meant to hold."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A one-off instruction relevant to only the current request — for example, 'summarize this in exactly three bullet points this time' — belongs in:",o:["An MCP prompt template, which is meant for reuse, not a one-off need","A project-level configuration file, unrelated to a single request","The system prompt, where it would persist across unrelated future turns","The current user turn, since it applies to this request specifically"],c:[3],e:"Task-specific, single-request instructions belong with that request, not in a persistent, conversation-wide location."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"What's the risk of putting a one-off, task-specific instruction into the system prompt instead of the current user turn?",o:["There's no risk at all — placement never has any practical effect","It can bleed into unrelated later turns, since the system prompt persists across the session","It automatically gets converted into a tool description","It will be silently ignored by the model regardless of where it's placed"],c:[1],e:"Because the system prompt persists, a one-off instruction placed there can wrongly keep applying to later, unrelated turns."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A subagent is dispatched to do broad, exploratory research with many dead ends, while a second subagent just needs to look up one deterministic fact with a known answer. Which is the better candidate for context isolation?",o:["Neither — isolation only ever applies to subtasks that use vision","The deterministic lookup, since its single fixed answer benefits most from isolation","The broad, exploratory research subtask, since isolation shields the main context from its noise","Both equally, since isolation provides identical benefit regardless of a subtask's shape"],c:[2],e:"Isolation earns its keep on noisy, exploratory work; a simple, fixed-answer lookup has little exploratory noise to shield against."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Requesting structured (for example, JSON) output from the model is valuable primarily because:",o:["It guarantees the response will always exactly match the requested schema","It removes the need for any validation of the response","It eliminates sampling-driven output variability entirely","It supports more reliable downstream parsing, paired with actual validation"],c:[3],e:"Structure improves the odds of clean parsing, but validation is still necessary — it isn't a guarantee on its own."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"'Defensive parsing' of a model's output means:",o:["A technique that only applies when the response includes vision content","Handling malformed or unexpected output gracefully instead of assuming perfect compliance","Disabling structured-output requests altogether to simplify the code","Trusting that a structured-output request guarantees exact compliance every time"],c:[1],e:"Defensive parsing anticipates imperfect compliance rather than assuming a requested format is always honored exactly."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Including several worked examples in a prompt, rather than only describing the desired output format in words, is primarily useful because:",o:["Verbal descriptions of format are not supported by the API at all","Including examples disables sampling-driven output variability","Examples always consume fewer tokens than an equivalent verbal description","Concrete examples often communicate formatting and style more reliably than prose alone"],c:[3],e:"Few-shot examples tend to pin down format/style more concretely than prose description alone, which is their main value."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Iterative prompt refinement refers to:",o:["Writing a prompt a single time and never revisiting it afterward","Adjusting a prompt over time based on real, observed failures and outputs","Refining only the model tier used, while leaving the prompt itself fixed","A process that only begins after a feature has publicly launched, with no earlier iteration"],c:[1],e:"Refinement is an ongoing, evidence-driven process grounded in what actually goes wrong in practice."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A prompt mostly performs well but consistently fails on one specific, identifiable edge case. What does this suggest?",o:["The model tier must be the cause, independent of the prompt's content","The entire prompt should always be discarded and rewritten from nothing","The failure means the prompt cannot be improved through iteration at all","A targeted refinement addressing that specific case, not a full rewrite"],c:[3],e:"An isolated, identifiable failure calls for targeted refinement — a full rewrite is disproportionate to a narrow, specific gap."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Guidance on exactly how a specific tool should behave when the model calls it is best placed:",o:["Nowhere, since tools are assumed to need no behavioral guidance at all","Only in the very first user message of the conversation","Only in the system prompt, kept entirely separate from the tool's own definition","In that tool's own description, close to where the model decides whether and how to call it"],c:[3],e:"Tool-specific behavior is most effectively communicated in that tool's own description, not somewhere disconnected from it."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Periodically summarizing older turns of a long session to free up context budget is a technique primarily aimed at managing:",o:["Prompt injection, which concerns untrusted content rather than volume","Model version pinning, which is unrelated to session history","Context bloat, by trimming the volume of accumulated low-value history","Context drift specifically, which concerns fading adherence rather than volume"],c:[2],e:"Summarization directly targets the volume problem that defines bloat."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Re-stating a critical constraint partway through a very long agent session is a practical mitigation for:",o:["Context drift, by re-anchoring an instruction that might otherwise be losing influence","Prompt cache invalidation, which is a caching concern, not an adherence concern","Tool schema errors, which are a definition problem, not an adherence problem","Context bloat specifically, which concerns volume rather than fading adherence"],c:[0],e:"Re-anchoring counters the gradual fading of influence that defines drift, distinct from trimming volume for bloat."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A strict formatting instruction given only once, 40 turns earlier in a long conversation, risks being effectively forgotten. Why?",o:["The instruction is automatically promoted into the system prompt after enough turns","Earlier instructions can lose effective influence as a session accumulates more content","Old instructions cause the session to terminate immediately once buried deeply enough","Instructions never lose influence over a session regardless of how long it runs"],c:[1],e:"This buried-instruction problem is a concrete symptom of drift/dilution in a long-running session."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Why might a very long, unpruned conversation history degrade output quality even while the request still stays under the hard context-window limit?",o:["Long, unpruned history automatically disables tool use for the rest of the session","It can't — quality is unaffected as long as the request stays under the limit","Relevant details can get diluted among a large volume of low-value accumulated history","An unpruned history always triggers an outright error before the limit is reached"],c:[2],e:"Dilution of relevant signal can hurt quality well before you actually hit the hard token ceiling."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Two instructions conflict — one set in the system prompt, one given in the current user turn. What's the soundest approach?",o:["Always trust the system prompt over the user turn without exception","Let the model silently pick whichever instruction it prefers, with no oversight","Always trust whichever instruction is more recent, with no further review","Avoid creating the conflict by design, rather than relying on implicit precedence"],c:[3],e:"Well-designed prompts are structured to avoid contradictions rather than leaning on assumed, implicit resolution rules."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Placing stable, reusable content — background docs, instructions, examples — at the very start of a prompt serves which two purposes together?",o:["It disables caching for that content, since only the final block can ever be cached","It gives the model a clear frame for the task and lets prompt caching reuse that content","It is required only when vision content blocks are present in the prompt","It has no effect on either clarity or caching, since ordering is purely cosmetic"],c:[1],e:"Prompt structure and caching mechanics reward the same habit: stable content up front, both for clarity and for cache reuse."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"'Compaction' of a long conversation's history refers to:",o:["Compressing images specifically, with no effect on text-based turns","A caching optimization that has no relationship to conversation history","Condensing older turns into a shorter representation that preserves essential information","Deleting the entire conversation history with no summary retained at all"],c:[2],e:"Compaction condenses rather than discards — the goal is to keep what matters while shrinking the footprint."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A tool's description reads 'use this tool for math,' but the tool actually only performs currency conversion. This is best characterized as a failure of:",o:["Prompt and tool-description clarity, likely to cause missed or incorrect tool use","Model tier selection, which is unrelated to how a tool is described","The Message Batches API, which has no bearing on tool descriptions","Prompt caching, which is unrelated to what a description communicates"],c:[0],e:"A mismatched, vague description is a context/prompt-engineering clarity problem, not a tier or caching issue."},
{d:"D6",t:"m",q:"Which three are legitimate techniques for managing a long agent session's context health? (Select 3)",o:["Deliberately maximizing how much history accumulates in the main context","Periodically summarizing or pruning low-value older history","Isolating a noisy subtask's exploration into a separate subagent context","Re-anchoring a critical instruction partway through a long session"],c:[1,2,3],e:"Isolation, summarization/pruning, and re-anchoring are all genuine context-health techniques; deliberately maximizing accumulated history works against context health."},
{d:"D6",t:"m",q:"Which two subtasks are good candidates for context isolation via a subagent? (Select 2)",o:["Formatting an already-finalized answer for display","A noisy, multi-step tool-search process with a lot of trial and error","A single deterministic lookup with one known, fixed answer","A broad, open-ended research task with many dead ends"],c:[1,3],e:"Isolation earns its overhead on noisy, exploratory work; simple deterministic lookups and pure formatting have little noise worth isolating."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Three separate internal applications all need the same standardized access to a company's CRM data. What's the most maintainable way to provide that?",o:["Having each application read CRM data through screenshots and vision","Building the same custom integration three times, once per application","A single MCP server exposing the CRM's resources and tools once, for all three apps","Copy-pasting the same integration code across all three codebases"],c:[2],e:"A shared MCP server avoids triplicating the same integration logic across otherwise-unrelated applications."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"A tool's description field mainly serves to:",o:["Reduce token cost automatically, independent of the tool's actual use","Enable streaming for that specific tool's responses","Help the model decide when and how to correctly invoke that tool","Provide end-user-facing product documentation, unrelated to model behavior"],c:[2],e:"The description is read by the model itself when deciding whether and how to call the tool — it's not user-facing documentation."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"A tool description is vague and generic, giving the model little to go on. What's the most likely consequence?",o:["The tool's result being cached automatically regardless of description quality","Guaranteed correct behavior, since any description at all satisfies the schema","Noticeably faster responses across the board, since less description means less to process","The model misusing the tool, or failing to call it when it actually would have been appropriate"],c:[3],e:"Description quality directly affects correct tool selection and invocation — vagueness tends to cause exactly this kind of misuse."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Client-side tools execute:",o:["Within your own application's environment, not on Anthropic's infrastructure","Only when a request is submitted through the Message Batches API","Entirely on Anthropic's own servers, identical to server-side tools","Only inside a browser context, never in a backend service"],c:[0],e:"The client-side/server-side distinction is defined by where execution actually happens — client-side runs in your own environment."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Server-side (or 'server') tools execute:",o:["Only in local development, never in production deployments","Within Anthropic's own infrastructure, rather than in the calling application's environment","Only when an MCP server is also connected to the same request","Only when manually triggered by an end user, never by the model itself"],c:[1],e:"Server tools are executed on Anthropic's side, which is the defining contrast with client-side tools."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"An 'approval pattern' applied to a tool that sends payments means:",o:["The model refuses to discuss payments in any context, unconditionally","A confirmation or human-in-the-loop step is required before the payment proceeds","The tool is permanently disabled and can never be called at all","The tool executes silently every time, with no additional review step"],c:[1],e:"An approval pattern gates the sensitive action behind a required confirmation, rather than removing the capability entirely."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"An MCP 'resource' primarily represents:",o:["Data that the server exposes for the model or client to read","A model tier setting applied to requests through that server","An executable action the model can trigger to change something","A billing configuration tied to the MCP server"],c:[0],e:"Resources are the data-access category in MCP, distinct from tools, which represent executable actions."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"An MCP 'prompt' primarily represents:",o:["An image content block passed through the MCP connection","A cached response stored for reuse across sessions","A tool's error message returned after a failed call","A reusable prompt template that the server provides to connecting clients"],c:[3],e:"MCP prompts are reusable templates a server can hand to clients, a distinct category from both resources and tools."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"An MCP server intended to run as a local subprocess, communicating with the client over standard input and output, uses which transport?",o:["WebSocket, which is not one of MCP's defined standard transports","gRPC, which is not one of MCP's defined standard transports","stdio","Streamable HTTP, which is meant for remote servers, not local subprocesses"],c:[2],e:"stdio is the transport for a locally-run MCP server communicating over standard input/output."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Streamable HTTP, MCP's transport for servers that run as independent remote processes, is best understood as:",o:["A transport limited to local, same-machine communication only, like stdio","The transport that replaced the legacy SSE approach for remote MCP servers over HTTP","A vision-specific transport unrelated to how tools or resources are served","A transport that MCP does not actually define or support"],c:[1],e:"Streamable HTTP is the current, defined transport for remote MCP servers, succeeding the earlier SSE-based approach."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"An MCP server needs to be reachable by clients running on entirely different machines, not just a local process on the same machine as the client. Which transport fits that requirement?",o:["stdio, which is designed for a local subprocess sharing the client's machine","A vision content block, which is unrelated to server transport","Neither transport supports remote access under any circumstances","Streamable HTTP, since it supports remote server processes handling multiple client connections"],c:[3],e:"Remote reachability across machines is exactly the case Streamable HTTP is designed for, unlike the local-only stdio transport."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Skills differ from MCP servers primarily in that Skills:",o:["Are functionally identical to MCP servers, with no meaningful distinction between them","Package reusable instructions and procedural knowledge for the model itself","Only work when a remote server is available, unlike MCP","Cannot be used alongside MCP servers in the same session"],c:[1],e:"Skills are model-facing instruction packages; MCP servers are external-system connectors — different mechanisms serving different needs."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Agent Skills use a 'progressive disclosure' architecture. At the first level, what gets loaded into context for every available Skill, regardless of whether it's used?",o:["The Skill's entire SKILL.md file contents, in full, for every installed Skill","Nothing at all — no Skill content is loaded until it is explicitly invoked by name","Just its metadata — the name and description from the SKILL.md frontmatter","All of the Skill's bundled scripts and reference files, loaded upfront"],c:[2],e:"Only the lightweight metadata is always loaded; the fuller instructions and bundled resources load later, only when actually needed."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Because only a Skill's metadata is loaded by default, what does this enable in practice?",o:["Only one Skill may ever be installed at a time, due to context limits","Many Skills can be installed simultaneously at minimal context cost","Skills must be manually loaded in full before every single request","Skills cannot bundle any scripts or reference files beyond SKILL.md itself"],c:[1],e:"Progressive disclosure is precisely what lets you install many Skills without paying their full context cost until one is actually relevant."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"A capability that's common, already solved well, and needs zero custom build effort — such as general web search — is best sourced from:",o:["Extended thinking mode, which is unrelated to sourcing external capabilities","A brand-new MCP server, built from scratch for this specific application","A built-in tool, maintained and implemented by Anthropic","A brand-new custom tool, built from scratch for this specific application"],c:[2],e:"Built-in tools exist precisely to cover common, pre-solved needs without requiring custom build work."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"A tool call fails because the external service it depends on times out. What should the integration do?",o:["Return an empty response with no indication that anything went wrong","Crash the entire application process immediately on any tool failure","Implement defined error handling so the failure is surfaced and handled","Permanently disable that tool for all future sessions after one timeout"],c:[2],e:"A handled, visible failure path is the correct response — not silence, a full crash, or a disproportionate permanent disablement."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"A tool performs an irreversible action, such as permanently deleting a record. What should generally gate that call?",o:["No special handling beyond what any ordinary, reversible tool call gets","An approval or confirmation step required before the action is allowed to execute","The tool being disabled at all times, making it unusable","Converting the tool into an MCP resource instead of a tool"],c:[1],e:"Irreversible actions warrant a confirmation gate; simply treating them like any other call skips a meaningful safeguard."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"An agent is given a single, extremely broad 'do anything' tool instead of several narrow, well-described tools. What's the main downside of that design?",o:["It automatically converts the tool into an MCP server","It always improves performance, with no meaningful downside","It disables streaming for the rest of the session","Reduced predictability and harder-to-reason-about behavior"],c:[3],e:"An overly broad tool undermines both predictability and the ability to scope permissions tightly — a real, named downside."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"The same MCP server needs to be used by both a command-line tool and a separate web application. What does MCP's standardization specifically enable here?",o:["Both clients can connect using the same protocol without needing fully custom, per-client integration work","Only vision-capable clients are able to connect to an MCP server","Only the command-line tool is actually able to connect to an MCP server","Nothing in particular — each client still needs a fully custom integration built from scratch"],c:[0],e:"Standardization is precisely what lets different kinds of clients reuse the same server without bespoke integration work each time."},
{d:"D8",t:"m",q:"Alongside tools, which two categories can an MCP server expose to connecting clients? (Select 2)",o:["Billing plans","UI theme settings","Resources","Prompts"],c:[2,3],e:"Resources and prompts are the two additional named categories MCP servers can expose beyond tools — billing and UI theming aren't part of the protocol."},
{d:"D8",t:"m",q:"Which three things should a well-written tool description clearly communicate? (Select 3)",o:["The internal org chart of the company that built it","What kind of result or output it returns","What inputs it expects","When the tool should be used"],c:[1,2,3],e:"Usage guidance, expected inputs, and expected output are the substantive content a tool description needs — an org chart is irrelevant to correct tool use."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Untrusted content retrieved from an external website is about to be fed into a prompt. What's the correct defensive approach?",o:["Add a system-prompt line politely asking the model to ignore any instructions found in that content","Trust the content fully, since Claude is assumed safe by default regardless of source","Disable tool use permanently for every future session as a precaution","Treat the content as untrusted, isolate it, and enforce guardrails against sensitive actions"],c:[3],e:"A polite request in the system prompt isn't an enforceable control; real defense requires isolation and guardrails around untrusted content."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"'Least privilege' applied to an agent's tool permissions means:",o:["Granting admin-level access by default, to be safe in case it's needed later","Disabling all tools entirely, regardless of the task","Granting every available tool by default, for convenience","Granting only the specific tools and permissions strictly required for the task at hand"],c:[3],e:"Least privilege scopes an agent's permissions tightly to what the task actually requires — not more, and not zero."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"A hook requires explicit confirmation before a file-deletion tool is allowed to execute. What is this an example of?",o:["A hook-based guardrail preventing a destructive action from running unreviewed","A prompt caching optimization applied to file operations","A model tier setting that only applies to destructive tools","An MCP resource definition unrelated to tool execution"],c:[0],e:"This is a lifecycle-based safety control gating a destructive action, not a caching or tier-related mechanism."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Which practice reflects sound handling of API keys used to call Claude?",o:["Logging the key in plaintext to make debugging easier","Embedding the key directly in a mobile app's client bundle for simplicity","Including the key in the system prompt for transparency with the model","Keeping keys out of prompts and client-side code, using proper secret storage"],c:[3],e:"Proper secret storage and avoiding client-side exposure are the sound baseline practices for handling API keys."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"An attacker crafts a cleverly worded message attempting to get the model to bypass its safety behavior. What's the most robust way to defend a production agent against this?",o:["Writing a longer, more emphatic system prompt asking the model not to comply","Relying solely on the model's built-in safety training, with no additional application-level controls","Defense in depth: application-level guardrails layered alongside the model's own training","Disabling all logging, so any incidents are less visible to reviewers"],c:[2],e:"Multiple independent layers of defense are far more robust against jailbreak attempts than model training alone."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Where should content policy enforcement happen for a production application, beyond whatever the model itself does?",o:["Nowhere — content policy enforcement is unnecessary overhead for most applications","Only for requests that happen to include vision content","At the application layer as well, rather than relying solely on the model's own judgment","Only inside the model's training process, with no application-side checks at all"],c:[2],e:"Application-layer enforcement is an essential additional control point, not a redundant one."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"An agent has an unrestricted 'send email' tool, and a prompt-injected webpage instructs it to email an attacker-controlled address. What combination of failures does this illustrate?",o:["A prompt caching misconfiguration unrelated to tool permissions or content handling","A Message Batches API misconfiguration unrelated to real-time tool execution","A lack of least-privilege scoping combined with poor isolation of untrusted content","A vision-specific limitation that has nothing to do with email tools"],c:[2],e:"This scenario combines two separate, real failure modes: unscoped tool permissions and untrusted-content isolation."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Why are short-lived credentials generally preferred over long-lived static API keys for a production agent?",o:["They are always cheaper to issue than long-lived keys, independent of any security benefit","They limit the window of exposure and potential damage if a credential is ever compromised","They are a strict requirement before streaming responses can be enabled","They directly improve the model's reasoning accuracy on the task"],c:[1],e:"The core benefit is a shorter exposure window, which limits how much damage a compromised credential can cause."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"A user pastes a document containing hidden text instructing the assistant to reveal the system prompt's contents. What's the correct mitigation category?",o:["Disable the chat feature entirely as the only viable response","Ignore the issue, treating it as too rare an edge case to address","Trust the document fully, since it was provided directly by the user rather than fetched from the web","Treat pasted document content as untrusted input, isolated from trusted instructions"],c:[3],e:"User-supplied documents are still external content and should be treated as untrusted, regardless of how they entered the conversation."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Why does least-privilege scoping matter specifically for an agent's tool permissions, beyond ordinary user-account permission hygiene?",o:["It only matters for agents that happen to use vision content blocks","It doesn't matter any differently for agents than for any other kind of software account","An agent's tool choices can be influenced by injected content, so scoping limits the worst case","It fully replaces the need for any other security control on its own"],c:[2],e:"Because an agent's actions can be swayed by content it reads, tight scoping bounds the potential damage from that influence."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"What's the problem with relying only on a model's built-in safety training as the sole defense for a production agent with access to sensitive tools?",o:["Nothing — model training alone is fully sufficient for any production deployment","It makes the agent noticeably slower to respond to every request","It disables tool use for the agent entirely by default","Training-level safety isn't an enforceable, application-level control"],c:[3],e:"Model training alone provides no enforceable, application-side ceiling on what a manipulated session might try to do."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Storing an API key in a `.env` file that's excluded from version control and loaded only at runtime is an example of:",o:["A poor practice that should generally be avoided in favor of hardcoding","Reasonable, standard secret-management practice","A prompt-caching technique unrelated to how secrets are stored","An MCP resource definition, unrelated to secret handling"],c:[1],e:"Excluding secrets from version control and loading them at runtime is a widely accepted, sound baseline practice."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"A hook blocks a 'send email' tool call unless the recipient's domain matches an approved allowlist. What does this hook accomplish?",o:["A model tier setting scoped to email-sending tasks","A caching optimization applied specifically to outbound email requests","An enforceable guardrail that limits the potential blast radius if the agent is compromised or misled","An unnecessary restriction that should generally be removed to simplify the integration"],c:[2],e:"This is a concrete, enforceable guardrail — exactly the kind of control that bounds worst-case damage."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Why are short-lived, narrowly scoped credentials preferable to a single long-lived admin-level API key for an agent that uses multiple tools?",o:["They increase the total token usage of every request the agent makes","They reduce both the exposure window and the scope of potential damage","They are required before streaming can be used with that agent","They provide no real benefit over a single long-lived admin key"],c:[1],e:"Both a shorter exposure window and a narrower scope of access reduce the potential blast radius of a compromised credential."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Content policy enforcement handled purely at the model-training level, with no checks added at the application layer, is risky mainly because:",o:["It leaves the application with no control point for stricter, deployment-specific rules","It always makes every response noticeably slower to generate","It is the officially recommended approach, with no meaningful downside","It disables tool use for the application by default"],c:[0],e:"Without an application-layer control point, a deployment loses the ability to layer on context-specific rules beyond what training alone provides."},
{d:"D7",t:"m",q:"Which two are enforceable technical controls against prompt injection risk? (Select 2)",o:["A politely worded system-prompt request asking the model to refuse injected instructions","Increasing max_tokens on the affected requests","Scoping tool permissions to least privilege","Isolating untrusted content from trusted instructions"],c:[2,3],e:"Content isolation and permission scoping are genuinely enforceable; a polite request and a token-limit change are not real technical controls against injection."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"Claude Code's headless mode is best described as:",o:["A billing tier rather than a way of running the tool","A mode restricted to vision-based tasks exclusively","An interactive-only mode that requires constant human input at every step","A non-interactive, scriptable mode suited to automation and CI-style pipelines"],c:[3],e:"Headless mode is designed for non-interactive, scripted use rather than a live back-and-forth session."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"CLAUDE.md files can be nested within subdirectories of a project. What does that nesting primarily enable?",o:["Disabling settings.json for that part of the project entirely","Faster raw API response times for requests touching that subdirectory","More specific, subdirectory-level guidance layered on top of root-level context","Automatic pinning of the model version used within that subdirectory"],c:[2],e:"Nesting lets guidance get more specific closer to the code it applies to, cascading on top of root-level context."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"A Skill authored for use in Claude Code packages:",o:["Reusable, task-specific instructions and best practices Claude can draw on","A fixed model tier setting applied to every session","An MCP transport protocol definition","Billing configuration for the current project"],c:[0],e:"Skills package procedural knowledge for reuse, distinct from billing, tier, or transport configuration."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"Where do custom Skills typically live when used in Claude Code?",o:["Stored only as environment variables, with no file-based representation","Embedded directly inside settings.json as inline JSON","Uploaded exclusively through a separate Skills API, with no filesystem-based option","As directories containing a SKILL.md file, in a personal or project-level folder"],c:[3],e:"Claude Code Skills are filesystem-based, living in personal or project-scoped folders that Claude reads directly."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"Claude Code's 'Agent Memory' is best described as:",o:["A cache of raw API responses, unrelated to task context","A vision-processing feature unrelated to session context","Persisted context or notes carrying relevant information across sessions","A billing ledger tracking token spend"],c:[2],e:"Agent Memory is about persisting useful context over time, not caching responses or tracking billing."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"A custom slash command in Claude Code is used to:",o:["Disable every configured hook for the session","Trigger a predefined, reusable action or prompt with a short command","Permanently delete the current project's files","Change the underlying model's training weights"],c:[1],e:"Slash commands are a shortcut for invoking a predefined, reusable action or prompt, not a destructive or model-altering operation."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"A permission mode that lets Claude Code take permitted actions on its own, without pausing for confirmation on each one, still operates:",o:["Only for read-only actions, never for actions that modify anything","Within whatever permission bounds have been configured for that session","With no permission bounds of any kind once that mode is enabled","Only when no hooks are configured for the session"],c:[1],e:"Autonomous action-taking still happens inside configured permission bounds — it isn't a bypass of permissions altogether."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"Where should permission and tool-access configuration for a Claude Code project generally live?",o:["A separate MCP server, unrelated to local permission configuration","The system prompt exclusively, with no dedicated configuration file","settings.json","CLAUDE.md, which is meant for project guidance rather than permissions"],c:[2],e:"settings.json is the configuration home for permissions, hooks, and tool access, distinct from CLAUDE.md's guidance role."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"A failure trace shows the model received malformed context because of a bug in how the application assembled the prompt. How should this be classified?",o:["A model output quality issue, unrelated to how the prompt was assembled","A security incident specifically, regardless of what actually caused it","An unfixable problem with no available path to resolution","An integration-layer bug, not a genuine model failure"],c:[3],e:"Correctly isolating an integration bug from a genuine model-output problem is the core skill trace analysis is meant to support."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"When debugging a production LLM feature, what should generally happen before choosing a fix?",o:["Isolating whether the failure originates in the integration layer or the model's output","Memorizing every possible error code the API could ever return","Ignoring the failure if it doesn't reproduce on the very next attempt","Retrying indefinitely regardless of what kind of error occurred"],c:[0],e:"Correct diagnosis before a matched recovery strategy is the core discipline behind effective debugging here."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"A regression appears in only 1 out of 20 runs of the exact same prompt. What should you consider before assuming it's a deterministic bug?",o:["Whether this is expected sampling-driven variability rather than a reproducible, deterministic defect","That vision content must be involved, regardless of whether the prompt uses any","That the API must be broken and should be reported immediately without further investigation","That the Message Batches API must be the cause, regardless of how the request was sent"],c:[0],e:"Low-frequency, intermittent variation is often expected non-determinism rather than a deterministic bug worth chasing as one."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"When a tool call fails, what's usually the first useful debugging step?",o:["Disabling tool use permanently as the default response to any failure","Using a trace to isolate whether the failure is in your code or in the model's request","Rewriting the entire application from scratch before investigating further","Switching model tiers immediately, without first investigating the cause"],c:[1],e:"Trace-based isolation of where the failure actually originates should come before any fix is chosen."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"A 'fallback strategy' for a failed model call might reasonably include:",o:["Silently ignoring the failure with no awareness of its impact on the user","Disabling logging so the issue becomes less visible going forward","Retrying with backoff, or falling back to a simpler deterministic path","Always crashing the application immediately, regardless of failure type"],c:[2],e:"A sound fallback strategy matches its response to the failure type, rather than defaulting to silence or a full crash."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"An eval suite built for an LLM feature should primarily measure:",o:["Only whether the surrounding code runs without throwing an exception","The total number of lines of code in the feature's implementation","Whether outputs meet defined quality and correctness criteria across representative cases","Which font is used in the feature's user interface"],c:[2],e:"Evals exist to check output quality against defined criteria, which goes well beyond just confirming the code executes."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"Which is a good candidate for an automated check to run in CI for an LLM feature?",o:["A one-time check performed only before the feature's very first launch","Disabling the existing test suite to save time on each build","A manual review of every single production request by a human reviewer","A structured-output schema-validation check run on sampled prompts on every change"],c:[3],e:"An automated, repeatable schema-validation check is exactly the kind of eval that fits naturally into a CI pipeline."},
];

if (typeof module !== "undefined" && module.exports) {
  module.exports = { DOMAINS, QUESTIONS };
}